.TH PRTEST 1
.SH NAME
prtest \- regression test for pr(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B prtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Prtest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/pr.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(argv) ,
and checks it against a real, live oracle: plan9port's own
.B pr
binary, run through this emu's host-command-execution device
.RB ( #C ,
see
.IR cmd (3))
on the identical file this same case wrote under
.BR /prtest ,
with the identical flags. Both outputs are normalised (see below) and
compared; every fixture is cleaned up whether or not its case fails.
.PP
This is the first p9/cmd test in this tree to compare against a real
reference binary automatically, on every run, rather than a hand-built
fixture or a one-off manual check: Limbo has no fork/exec of its own
(checked directly against
.BR module/sys.m ),
but this emu exposes a real host-command-execution device anyway (see
.IR cmd (3)),
and
.B runhost()
below drives it, adapted from
.BR appl/cmd/os.b 's
own working use of the same device. Host commands run in the HOST's
own filesystem namespace, not Inferno's, so
.B hostroot()
first runs
.B /bin/pwd
through the same device to discover the real host path this checkout
lives at, dynamically, rather than assuming one.
.PP
.B Normalize():
the only genuine, expected difference between the two outputs is the
page header's own date/time \(em this emu's
.B Daytime
module and the host's real
.B ctime()
may resolve different time zones, a real environmental fact, not a
bug in either binary (see
.IR pr (1)'s
own BUGS). Every header line has everything up to its own first
double-space replaced with a fixed placeholder before comparing,
leaving the filename/"Page N" portion \(em which DOES still get
compared \(em intact.
.PP
.B T4
exercises the real
.BR \-n / \-b
fallthrough bug documented in
.IR pr (1)'s
own BUGS directly: a 20-line file in 3 columns with a short page
length, once plain (last page ragged) and once with
.B \-n
added and no
.B \-b
at all (last page balanced anyway). Confirmed sensitive with a
genuine negative control during development: temporarily breaking
.BR balance ()'s
own line-skip loop (it must advance one character past each
newline it finds, matching the original's
.B "while(*s++ != '\en')"
post-increment \(em Limbo has no post-increment operator, so this is
its own explicit statement) made this case's output truncate to a
single line partway through the first page, restored afterward.
.PP
.B T13
and
.B T14
exercise
.BR get() 's
.B Etabn/peekc
input-tab-expansion branch, flagged by review as the trickiest
untested state in the file: on a tab character,
.B peekc
is set to a
.I count
.RI ( Inpos%Etabn ),
not a flag, and the next call to
.B get()
tests it as a boolean and clears it \(em so one tab expands to either
one or two output characters depending on where it fell. T13 runs a
single-column file (where
.B Etabn
stays 0 unless
.B \-e
is given) both plain and with
.B \-e4
explicit; T14 runs the same tab-bearing content through two columns,
where
.IR pr (1)'s
own
.B findopt()
forces
.B Etabn=DEFTAB
even with no
.B \-e
given at all, so the same branch fires implicitly too.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/prtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR pr (1)
.SH BUGS
Does not exercise
.B \-i
(output tab compression) beyond its default, formfeed combined with
multi-column output, or the empty-merge-set failure path
.RI ( pr (1)'s
own BUGS on merge mode's all-or-nothing behaviour) \(em all
translated in
.BR pr.b ,
none independently verified to the same depth as the cases above.
.PP
.B T1
is the only case run without
.B \-t
(so the header, including the filename, is actually compared, not
just normalised away) \(em it
.B chdir()s
into
.B /prtest
and uses a bare filename on both sides specifically so the two
processes report the identical name in their own headers, since
neither side strips or rewrites the path it's given.
